<ArticleLayout
  title="Condensation and Mould During Winter: What to Check Before It Spreads"
  seoTitle="Winter Condensation and Mould | Australian Home Guide"
  description="Find the causes of winter condensation and mould. Check moisture sources, windows, heating, ventilation, insulation, leaks and safe cleaning before buying products."
  category="Living Spaces"
  date="Published July 2026"
  datePublished="2026-07-29"
  readingTime="28 minute read"
  showAffiliateDisclosure={false}
  image="/images/condensation-and-mould-during-winter.webp"
  imageAlt="Winter condensation on an Australian home window with a small area of mould beginning near the frame"
  socialImage="/images/condensation-and-mould-during-winter.webp"
  socialImageAlt="Winter condensation on an Australian home window with a small area of mould beginning near the frame"
  imagePosition="67% center"
>
  <p>Condensation is easy to dismiss when it appears as a little water on the <a href="/categories/bedroom/">bedroom window</a>.</p>
  <p>The concern is what happens repeatedly.</p>
  <p>Moisture can collect on glass, frames, walls and behind furniture each cold morning. Paint begins to discolour, wardrobes smell musty and mould returns soon after it has been wiped away.</p>
  <p>Cleaning the visible growth may be necessary, but it does not explain why the surface remains damp.</p>
  <p><a href="/seasonal/">Winter condensation</a> usually involves three conditions:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li><a href="/categories/living-spaces/">moisture inside the home</a></li>
    <li>a surface cold enough for that moisture to condense</li>
    <li>insufficient ventilation or drying for the surface to recover</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A leak can create similar signs and may occur at the same time, but it requires a different repair.</p>
  <p>This guide explains how to separate likely causes, reduce indoor moisture and recognise when the problem needs more than another cleaning product.</p>
  <h2>Our Take</h2>
  <p>Find the water before treating the stain.</p>
  <p>A window that is wet every morning, mould behind a wardrobe or recurring discolouration on a ceiling is evidence of a moisture problem.</p>
  <p>The useful questions are:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>Where is the moisture coming from?</li>
    <li>Why is this surface becoming cold or staying wet?</li>
    <li>Can the room ventilate and dry?</li>
    <li>Is water entering through a building defect?</li>
    <li>Is the affected material still sound and cleanable?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Wiping the surface without changing those conditions usually means the mould returns.</p>
  <h2>What is condensation?</h2>
  <p>Air contains water vapour.</p>
  <p>Warm air can hold more water vapour than cooler air. When warm, moisture-laden indoor air meets a sufficiently cold surface, some of that water vapour can become liquid.</p>
  <p>This is condensation.</p>
  <p>Common winter surfaces include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>window glass</li>
    <li>aluminium window frames</li>
    <li>external walls</li>
    <li>room corners</li>
    <li>ceilings beneath cold roof spaces</li>
    <li>uninsulated wall sections</li>
    <li>the rear of wardrobes</li>
    <li>walls behind beds and sofas</li>
    <li>cold water pipes</li>
    <li>skylights</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The temperature at which condensation begins is called the dew point.</p>
  <p>The dew point changes with the air temperature and the amount of moisture present. This is why condensation cannot be solved by one universal humidity number or one appliance setting.</p>
  <h2>Surface and concealed condensation</h2>
  <h3>Surface condensation</h3>
  <p>Surface condensation is visible on the room side of a material.</p>
  <p>Examples include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>water droplets on glass</li>
    <li>damp window sills</li>
    <li>mould at frame corners</li>
    <li>moisture on tiled walls</li>
    <li>dark spots behind furniture</li>
    <li>dampness inside a wardrobe</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Interstitial condensation</h3>
  <p>Interstitial condensation occurs within the building assembly, such as inside:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>walls</li>
    <li>ceilings</li>
    <li>roofs</li>
    <li>floors</li>
    <li>insulation layers</li>
    <li>cavities</li>
  </ul>
  <p>It may remain hidden until there is:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>staining</li>
    <li>mould</li>
    <li>corrosion</li>
    <li>damaged insulation</li>
    <li>softened plasterboard</li>
    <li>timber decay</li>
    <li>peeling paint</li>
    <li>a musty smell</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Concealed condensation requires building-specific assessment. Do not open walls or ceilings simply to look for it.</p>
  <h2>Condensation, damp and mould at a glance</h2>
  <p class="table-hint">Swipe sideways to compare the full table.</p>
  <div class="comparison-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Condensation, damp and mould at a glance comparison">
    <table class="comparison-table">
      <caption>Condensation, damp and mould at a glance</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">What you notice</th><th scope="col">Possible causes to investigate</th><th scope="col">Do not assume</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Water on bedroom windows each morning</td><td>Moist indoor air meeting cold glass, closed room, limited ventilation or weak window performance</td><td>The window necessarily leaks</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mould at window corners</td><td>Repeated condensation, trapped dust, damaged seals or poor airflow</td><td>Stronger cleaner will stop it returning</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mould behind a wardrobe</td><td>Cold external wall, limited air circulation, high humidity or water entry</td><td>Moving the wardrobe once solves the moisture source</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Damp ceiling patch</td><td>Roof leak, plumbing leak, condensation or failed exhaust ducting</td><td>It is definitely condensation</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mould on bathroom ceiling</td><td>Inadequate exhaust, cold surface, long showers, failed duct or roof leak</td><td>Repainting is sufficient</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Musty wardrobe</td><td>Damp clothing, cold external wall, poor circulation, leak or high room humidity</td><td>Fragrance products remove the cause</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Water beneath a window</td><td>Heavy condensation, blocked window drainage, damaged seals or external water entry</td><td>Every gap should be sealed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Peeling paint near an external wall</td><td>Condensation, rising or penetrating damp, leak or incompatible coating</td><td>More paint will repair it</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mould around an air conditioner</td><td>Condensation, blocked drainage, dirty components, air leakage or building moisture</td><td>The appliance only needs fragrance treatment</td></tr>
