        <tr><td>Mould after furniture is moved</td><td>Long-term low airflow and cold surface conditions</td><td>The colour identifies how dangerous it is</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mould returning after cleaning</td><td>Moisture source remains or affected material cannot dry</td><td>The cleaning product was simply too weak</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>These signs are starting points rather than a remote diagnosis.</p>
  <p>More than one cause can be present.</p>
  <h2>Where indoor moisture comes from</h2>
  <p>Normal household activities add water vapour to the air.</p>
  <p>Sources include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>breathing</li>
    <li><a href="/categories/bathroom/">showering</a></li>
    <li>bathing</li>
    <li>cooking</li>
    <li>boiling water</li>
    <li>dishwashing</li>
    <li><a href="/categories/laundry/">washing clothes</a></li>
    <li>drying clothes indoors</li>
    <li>vented clothes dryers</li>
    <li>unflued gas heaters</li>
    <li>aquariums</li>
    <li>large numbers of indoor plants</li>
    <li>damp shoes and clothing</li>
    <li>wet cleaning</li>
    <li>water leaks</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Moisture becomes a problem when it is produced faster than the home can remove it or when it reaches cold surfaces before it can be exhausted.</p>
  <p>The aim is not to eliminate all indoor moisture. It is to control large sources and give the home an appropriate way to dry.</p>
  <h2>Why winter exposes the problem</h2>
  <p>Winter can increase condensation because:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>windows and external walls become colder</li>
    <li>doors and windows remain closed for longer</li>
    <li>occupants spend more time indoors</li>
    <li>showers may be hotter or longer</li>
    <li>clothes are more likely to dry inside</li>
    <li>rooms may be heated unevenly</li>
    <li>unoccupied rooms stay cold</li>
    <li>curtains remain closed</li>
    <li>furniture blocks air movement</li>
    <li>exhaust fans may be used for too little time</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Warm air from a heated living room can also move into a cold bedroom or spare room and condense on colder surfaces.</p>
  <p>A house does not need to feel humid for condensation to occur at a sufficiently cold surface.</p>
  <h2>Australia does not have one condensation pattern</h2>
  <p>Condensation risk changes with:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>climate</li>
    <li>season</li>
    <li>indoor air conditioning</li>
    <li>building construction</li>
    <li>heating</li>
    <li>cooling</li>
    <li>humidity</li>
    <li>membranes</li>
    <li>insulation</li>
    <li>ventilation</li>
  </ul>
  <p>In cool and temperate regions, winter condensation commonly involves indoor moisture moving towards cold external surfaces.</p>
  <p>In tropical and subtropical regions, condensation can also occur during warm or humid seasons when air conditioning cools internal materials below the outdoor air’s dew point.</p>
  <p>This guide focuses on <a href="/guides/preparing-your-home-for-winter/">winter household condensation</a>, but northern Australian homes should not assume that winter is their highest-risk period.</p>
  <p>Building membranes, vapour control and insulation must suit the climate and direction of moisture movement.</p>
  <h2>Windows</h2>
  <p>Windows are often the first place condensation becomes visible because glass and metal frames can become colder than the surrounding room.</p>
  <p>Check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>whether water forms across the complete pane or only one corner</li>
    <li>whether the frame is metal, timber, uPVC or another material</li>
    <li>whether seals are damaged</li>
    <li>whether the sash closes correctly</li>
    <li>whether drainage slots remain open</li>
    <li>whether curtains trap damp air against the glass</li>
    <li>whether the sill remains wet during the day</li>
    <li>whether water appears only after rain</li>
    <li>whether staining exists beneath the frame</li>
    <li>whether external sealant or flashing is damaged</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not seal window drainage slots or weep openings.</p>
  <p>Water entering only during wind-driven rain may indicate a weatherproofing fault rather than ordinary indoor condensation.</p>
  <h2>Wipe recurring window water</h2>
  <p>Visible condensation should not be left to soak into:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>timber frames</li>
    <li>paint</li>
    <li>plasterboard</li>
    <li>carpet</li>
    <li>blinds</li>
    <li>curtains</li>
    <li>window sills</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Wipe the water using a clean absorbent cloth and dry the surrounding surface.</p>
  <p>This manages the immediate moisture. It does not prevent the next occurrence.</p>
  <p>If wiping is required every morning, investigate:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>indoor moisture sources</li>
    <li>ventilation</li>
    <li>room heating</li>
    <li>window coverings</li>
    <li>glazing</li>
    <li>frame performance</li>
    <li>draughts</li>
    <li>building defects</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not allow a wet cloth to remain in the room where it adds the moisture back as it dries.</p>
  <h2>Curtains, blinds and pelmets</h2>
  <p><a href="/guides/bedroom-lighting-and-window-coverings-what-to-compare-before-you-buy/">Window coverings</a> can reduce heat loss and improve surface comfort.</p>
  <p>They can also trap moist air against cold glass if:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>curtains touch the window</li>
    <li>air cannot circulate</li>
    <li>wet fabric remains closed</li>
    <li>the sill is covered</li>
    <li>condensation is not wiped away</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Check whether coverings:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>remain clear of the glass</li>
    <li>can open for daytime drying</li>
    <li>cover the window effectively at night</li>
    <li>have mould or water marks</li>
    <li>can be cleaned according to their care instructions</li>
    <li>allow the window to open where ventilation is appropriate</li>
    <li>remain outside heater clearances</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A pelmet can reduce warm room air circulating behind the curtain, but the complete arrangement still needs to dry.</p>
  <p>Open curtains during the day where useful sunlight and safe ventilation can help.</p>
  <h2>Bedrooms</h2>
  <p>Bedrooms can accumulate moisture overnight through breathing while the room remains closed.</p>
  <p>Condensation risk can increase where:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>two or more people sleep in a small room</li>
    <li>the door and windows remain closed</li>
    <li>the room is unheated</li>
    <li>the window is single glazed</li>
    <li>heavy curtains remain closed all day</li>
    <li>furniture sits against an external wall</li>
    <li>an ensuite adds moisture</li>
    <li>damp clothing or towels remain inside</li>
    <li>electric bedding warms the bed but the room stays cold</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Check the room each morning for:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>wet windows</li>
    <li>damp sills</li>
    <li>musty odour</li>
    <li>mould behind the bedhead</li>
    <li>mould in wardrobe corners</li>
    <li>damp bedding</li>
    <li>water around an ensuite doorway</li>
  </ul>
  <p><a href="/guides/heating-a-bedroom-overnight-comfort/">A cold bedroom</a> may need improved ventilation and surface warmth, not simply a portable heater left operating overnight.</p>
  <h2>Furniture against external walls</h2>
  <p>Large furniture can restrict airflow and create a cold, still pocket behind it.</p>
  <p>Common examples include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>wardrobes</li>
    <li>bedheads</li>
    <li>sofas</li>
    <li>bookcases</li>
    <li>storage boxes</li>
    <li>desks</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Where practical, allow space between large furnishings and cold external walls.</p>
  <p>Do not stack cardboard boxes, clothing or soft furnishings tightly against a wall that repeatedly becomes damp.</p>
  <p>Inspect behind furniture periodically.</p>
  <p>Mould behind a wardrobe can indicate:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>condensation</li>
    <li>missing insulation</li>
    <li>a thermal bridge</li>
    <li>water entry</li>
    <li>damp items stored inside</li>
    <li>poor room ventilation</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Moving the furniture may help the surface dry, but the underlying cause still needs to be identified.</p>
  <h2>Wardrobes and cupboards</h2>
  <p>Wardrobes can remain colder and less ventilated than the room.</p>
  <p>Risk increases where:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the rear wall is external</li>
    <li>clothing is stored damp</li>
    <li>items are packed tightly</li>
    <li>doors remain closed</li>
    <li>shoes are wet</li>
    <li>cardboard boxes sit against the wall</li>
    <li>the room has high humidity</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Practical steps include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>ensuring clothes and shoes are fully dry before storage</li>
    <li>avoiding overfilling</li>
    <li>allowing airflow where practical</li>
    <li>leaving space from the rear wall</li>
    <li>removing damp cardboard</li>
    <li>checking corners and the floor</li>
    <li>reporting water entry</li>
    <li>cleaning only once the moisture source is addressed</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Moisture-absorbing products collect a limited amount of water. They do not repair leaks, insulate the wall or ventilate the room.</p>
  <h2>Bathrooms</h2>
  <p>Bathrooms can release a large amount of moisture quickly.</p>
  <p>Check whether the exhaust fan:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>starts when required</li>
    <li>moves air effectively</li>
    <li>remains on long enough after use</li>
    <li>exhausts outside as designed</li>
    <li>has a clear grille</li>
    <li>has an operating backdraft damper</li>
    <li>is obstructed by dust</li>
    <li>is appropriately sized and installed</li>
    <li>has damaged or disconnected ducting</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Use the exhaust fan during showers and baths and continue using it afterwards according to the system guidance.</p>
  <p>Keep the bathroom door positioned so moisture does not simply move into bedrooms and hallways.</p>
  <p>Wipe wet surfaces where practical.</p>
  <p>A bathroom fan that only moves moist air into the roof space can contribute to concealed moisture problems.</p>
  <h2>Kitchen</h2>
  <p>Cooking releases moisture through:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>boiling</li>
    <li>steaming</li>
    <li>simmering</li>
    <li>dishwashing</li>
    <li>kettles</li>
    <li>gas combustion</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Use lids where suitable and operate an effective rangehood or exhaust system during moisture-producing cooking.</p>
  <p>A recirculating rangehood filters some grease and odour but does not exhaust water vapour outside in the same way as a correctly ducted system.</p>
  <p>Check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>filter condition</li>
    <li>ducting</li>
    <li>exterior outlet</li>
    <li>backdraft damper</li>
    <li>air movement</li>
    <li>whether cupboards above the cooktop become damp</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not block a rangehood duct to reduce winter draughts.</p>
  <p>Where gas cooking is present, ventilation also affects indoor combustion pollutants.</p>
  <h2>Laundry</h2>
  <p>Laundry moisture may come from:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>wet washing</li>
    <li>indoor drying racks</li>
    <li>vented dryers</li>
    <li>washing-machine leaks</li>
    <li>utility sinks</li>
    <li>poor drainage</li>
    <li>cold external walls</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A vented dryer expels warm moist air into the room unless it is correctly vented outside.</p>
  <p>Check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>dryer type</li>
    <li>lint filters</li>
    <li>duct or hose condition</li>
    <li>exterior discharge</li>
    <li>window or exhaust ventilation</li>
    <li>condensate container or drain where applicable</li>
    <li>water beneath appliances</li>
    <li>visible hose connections</li>
    <li>wall and ceiling staining</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not assume every dryer handles moisture the same way.</p>
  <p>Condenser and heat-pump dryers manage moisture differently from vented dryers, but they still require maintenance and appropriate installation.</p>
  <h2>Drying clothes indoors</h2>
  <p><a href="/guides/laundry-drying-space-what-to-measure-before-buying-airers-or-racks/">Indoor drying</a> can add substantial moisture to the room.</p>
  <p>Where outdoor drying is not practical, consider:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>using an appropriately ventilated laundry</li>
    <li>operating the correct exhaust system</li>
    <li>using a dryer designed and installed for the space</li>
    <li>spacing clothes so they dry more quickly</li>
    <li>avoiding bedrooms and closed living rooms</li>
    <li>removing dry clothes promptly</li>
    <li>monitoring condensation</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not place drying racks within heater clearances.</p>
  <p>Do not drape laundry over heaters.</p>
  <p>A heated room can dry clothes while distributing that moisture through the home unless it is removed through ventilation or condensation control.</p>
  <h2>Leaks versus condensation</h2>
  <p>Condensation often follows occupancy patterns and cold conditions.</p>
  <p>A leak may be more likely where:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>dampness appears after rain</li>
    <li>one patch remains wet regardless of indoor activity</li>
    <li>paint bubbles</li>
    <li>plasterboard softens</li>
    <li>water stains spread</li>
    <li>timber swells</li>
    <li>water drips</li>
    <li>an odour persists during dry weather</li>
    <li>plumbing use triggers the problem</li>
    <li>the issue occurs beneath a bathroom, roof or pipe</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Possible water sources include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>roof leaks</li>
    <li>failed flashing</li>
    <li>plumbing</li>
    <li>shower waterproofing</li>
    <li>window leaks</li>
    <li>blocked gutters</li>
    <li>air-conditioner drainage</li>
    <li>rising or penetrating damp</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not describe every winter damp patch as condensation.</p>
  <p>Leaks should be repaired before damaged finishes or mould are treated as cosmetic issues.</p>
  <h2>Gutters and roof drainage</h2>
  <p><a href="/guides/gutter-maintenance-guide/">Blocked or defective gutters</a> can direct water into:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>eaves</li>
    <li>wall cavities</li>
    <li>ceilings</li>
    <li>window heads</li>
    <li>foundations</li>
  </ul>
  <p>From ground level, look for:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>overflow</li>
    <li>staining</li>
    <li>disconnected downpipes</li>
    <li>sagging gutters</li>
    <li>water pooling beside the house</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not climb onto the roof to investigate.</p>
  <p>Recurring ceiling or wall dampness after rain may require appropriate roofing, plumbing or drainage assessment.</p>
  <p>Cleaning indoor mould will not stop external water entry.</p>
  <h2>Plumbing and bathroom leaks</h2>
  <p>Possible signs include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>dampness near taps</li>
    <li>swelling cabinetry</li>
    <li>water beneath a vanity</li>
    <li>damaged silicone or grout</li>
    <li>moisture around a toilet</li>
    <li>wet carpet outside a bathroom</li>
    <li>a water meter moving unexpectedly</li>
    <li>repeated ceiling stains below wet areas</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not remove wall linings or plumbing fittings without appropriate knowledge and authority.</p>
  <p>A slow leak can keep materials damp while producing little visible water.</p>
  <p>Report or arrange investigation promptly.</p>
  <h2>Heating and surface temperature</h2>
  <p><a href="/guides/home-heating-options-australia/">Heating can raise</a> indoor air and surface temperatures, reducing the chance that some surfaces reach the dew point.</p>
  <p>Uneven heating can still leave:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>spare rooms</li>
    <li>wardrobes</li>
    <li>external corners</li>
    <li>window reveals</li>
    <li>rooms behind closed doors</li>
  </ul>
  <p>cold enough for condensation.</p>
  <p>Heating alone may not solve the problem if moisture production remains high.</p>
  <p>A very warm living room can also send moist air into a colder room, where it condenses.</p>
  <p>Use heating as one part of the strategy alongside:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>source ventilation</li>
    <li>controlled air exchange</li>
    <li>insulation</li>
    <li>window improvement</li>
    <li>draught management</li>
    <li>leak repair</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Portable heaters</h2>
  <p>Electric portable heaters do not add combustion moisture to the room.</p>
  <p>They also do not remove existing water vapour.</p>
  <p>Do not use an unflued gas heater as a mould-control method. Combustion can add moisture and pollutants to the home.</p>
  <p>Never bring outdoor gas heaters, barbecues or charcoal appliances indoors.</p>
  <p>Portable electric heaters must retain required clearances and should be switched off before leaving the room or going to sleep.</p>
  <h2>Reverse-cycle air conditioning</h2>
  <p>In heating mode, a <a href="/guides/reverse-cycle-heating-explained/">reverse-cycle system</a> warms the room but does not operate like cooling or dry mode.</p>
  <p>In cooling mode, the system can remove moisture as water condenses on the indoor coil.</p>
  <p>Dry mode can reduce humidity under suitable warm or humid conditions, but it may cool the room and is not a universal winter solution.</p>
  <p>Before using any mode, check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>manufacturer instructions</li>
    <li>indoor temperature</li>
    <li>outdoor conditions</li>
    <li>drainage</li>
    <li>filter condition</li>
    <li>whether the room becomes uncomfortably cold</li>
    <li>whether the moisture source remains</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not rely on an air conditioner to manage a roof or plumbing leak.</p>
  <h2>Dehumidifiers</h2>
  <p>A dehumidifier removes moisture from room air and collects or drains the water.</p>
  <p>It may help where:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>ventilation is difficult during wet weather</li>
    <li>indoor drying cannot be avoided</li>
    <li>a room remains humid</li>
    <li>the underlying building is otherwise dry</li>
    <li>the appliance suits the room and climate</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Before buying, check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>extraction performance at relevant temperatures</li>
    <li>operating noise</li>
    <li>collection-tank size</li>
    <li>drainage options</li>
    <li>filter access</li>
    <li>frost protection</li>
    <li>minimum operating temperature</li>
    <li>energy use</li>
    <li>clearances</li>
    <li>safe power-point access</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Some refrigerant dehumidifiers collect less water in cold rooms.</p>
  <p>A dehumidifier can manage indoor moisture. It cannot repair:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>leaking roofs</li>
    <li>failed waterproofing</li>
    <li>broken plumbing</li>
    <li>damp building materials</li>
    <li>inadequate drainage</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If the tank fills quickly every day, investigate where the moisture is coming from.</p>
  <h2>Humidity meters</h2>
  <p>A basic humidity meter can help identify patterns.</p>
  <p>Use it to compare:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>morning and afternoon</li>
    <li>occupied and unoccupied rooms</li>
    <li>before and after showers</li>
    <li>before and after ventilation</li>
    <li>different parts of the home</li>
    <li>wet and dry weather</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Consumer meters vary in accuracy.</p>
  <p>Treat the reading as useful context rather than a diagnosis.</p>
  <p>Do not respond to one number by sealing the home, running a humidifier or buying multiple appliances.</p>
  <p>Look at:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>visible condensation</li>
    <li>drying time</li>
    <li>temperature</li>
    <li>building condition</li>
    <li>moisture sources</li>
    <li>ventilation</li>
  </ul>
  <p>together.</p>
  <h2>Ventilation without losing all the heat</h2>
  <p>Winter ventilation needs to remove moisture without leaving the home permanently open to cold weather.</p>
  <p>Possible approaches include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>using bathroom and kitchen exhaust fans at the source</li>
    <li>short, purposeful window opening when outdoor conditions are suitable</li>
    <li>ventilating after showers, cooking or indoor drying</li>
    <li>using mechanical ventilation where installed</li>
    <li>keeping required vents clear</li>
    <li>closing the room again after the moisture has been removed</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Opening every window for hours is not the only form of ventilation.</p>
  <p>The most effective method depends on:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>climate</li>
    <li>weather</li>
    <li>security</li>
    <li>outdoor humidity</li>
    <li>smoke</li>
    <li>pollen</li>
    <li>building design</li>
    <li>mechanical systems</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Airtightness can improve thermal comfort, but tighter homes need reliable controlled ventilation.</p>
  <h2>Draught proofing and ventilation are not opposites</h2>
  <p><a href="/guides/reduce-draughts-before-buying-bigger-heater/">Draught proofing reduces</a> uncontrolled leakage.</p>
  <p>Ventilation provides controlled air exchange.</p>
  <p>A home can be:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>draughty but poorly ventilated in the rooms that need it</li>
    <li>well sealed and well ventilated</li>
    <li>tightly sealed with inadequate ventilation</li>
    <li>leaky and still mould affected</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not block:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>exhaust fans</li>
    <li>gas-appliance air supply</li>
    <li>window drainage</li>
    <li>subfloor vents</li>
    <li>weep holes</li>
    <li>evaporative cooling requirements</li>
    <li>unknown building openings</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Reduce avoidable gaps while preserving intentional ventilation.</p>
  <h2>Insulation</h2>
  <p><a href="/guides/home-insulation-basics/">Insulation helps</a> keep internal surfaces warmer during winter.</p>
  <p>This can reduce surface-condensation risk.</p>
  <p>Performance depends on:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>climate</li>
    <li>location</li>
    <li>continuity</li>
    <li>installation quality</li>
    <li>thermal bridges</li>
    <li>moisture</li>
    <li>framing</li>
    <li>roof and wall membranes</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Missing or compressed insulation can leave local cold spots where condensation develops.</p>
  <p>Do not enter a roof space casually to inspect it.</p>
  <p>Use existing records or arrange suitable professional assessment where required.</p>
  <p>Wet insulation should not simply be covered with another layer. The moisture source must be identified.</p>
  <h2>Thermal bridges</h2>
  <p>A thermal bridge is a building element that transfers heat more readily than the surrounding insulated area.</p>
  <p>Examples include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>steel framing</li>
    <li>concrete slab edges</li>
    <li>metal window frames</li>
    <li>structural beams</li>
    <li>uninsulated junctions</li>
    <li>poorly detailed corners</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Mould may appear in a repeated line or pattern corresponding with framing or junctions.</p>
  <p>Cleaning the pattern does not change the cold bridge.</p>
  <p>Addressing a thermal bridge may require renovation or professional building-envelope advice rather than a household surface product.</p>
  <h2>Glazing and frames</h2>
  <p>Single glazing and conductive metal frames can become cold quickly.</p>
  <p>Possible improvements range from:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>effective curtains and pelmets</li>
    <li>close-fitting blinds</li>
    <li>repair of seals</li>
    <li>secondary glazing</li>
    <li>insulated glazing</li>
    <li>improved frames</li>
    <li>replacement during renovation</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not assume window replacement is the first or only answer.</p>
  <p>Check whether the problem includes:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>air leakage</li>
    <li>failed seals</li>
    <li>poor drainage</li>
    <li>inadequate coverings</li>
    <li>high indoor moisture</li>
    <li>water entry</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A strong window treatment can improve comfort but should not trap untreated moisture against the frame.</p>
  <h2>Wall and roof membranes</h2>
  <p>Building membranes control the movement of:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>liquid water</li>
    <li>air</li>
    <li>water vapour</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The correct type and position depend on the climate and wall or roof construction.</p>
  <p>A vapour barrier or foil layer placed in the wrong position can increase condensation risk.</p>
  <p>Do not purchase or install membrane products from a generic online diagram.</p>
  <p>This work requires understanding:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>climate zone</li>
    <li>warm and cold sides of the assembly</li>
    <li>vapour permeability</li>
    <li>drainage</li>
    <li>cladding</li>
    <li>roofing</li>
    <li>insulation</li>
    <li>bushfire requirements</li>
    <li>manufacturer systems</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Membrane and cavity changes belong within a properly designed building assembly.</p>
  <h2>Mould and health</h2>
  <p>Mould can affect people differently.</p>
  <p>Possible reactions can include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>nasal irritation</li>
    <li>eye irritation</li>
    <li>coughing</li>
    <li>wheezing</li>
    <li>skin irritation</li>
    <li>worsening of asthma or allergies</li>
  </ul>
  <p>People with weakened immune systems or chronic lung conditions may require greater caution.</p>
  <p>This guide cannot determine whether a symptom is caused by mould.</p>
  <p>Seek medical advice where:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>symptoms are persistent</li>
    <li>breathing is affected</li>
    <li>asthma worsens</li>
    <li>a vulnerable household member is exposed</li>
    <li>health concerns continue after leaving the room</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Call Triple Zero (000) for an emergency.</p>
  <h2>Before cleaning mould</h2>
  <p>First identify and control the moisture source as far as practical.</p>
  <p>Do not begin <a href="/guides/spring-cleaning-checklist/">household cleaning</a> where there is:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>extensive growth</li>
    <li>recurring widespread growth</li>
    <li>sewage contamination</li>
    <li>flood contamination</li>
    <li>significant water damage</li>
    <li>soft or crumbling material</li>
    <li>suspected asbestos</li>
    <li>contaminated heating or ventilation systems</li>
    <li>mould inside wall or ceiling cavities</li>
    <li>a vulnerable person who should not be exposed</li>
    <li>unsafe access</li>
    <li>uncertainty about the material</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Small visible areas on sound, accessible hard surfaces may sometimes be managed using current state health guidance.</p>
  <p>The decision depends on the scale, surface, occupant and moisture source.</p>
  <h2>Cleaning boundaries</h2>
  <p>Where current health guidance supports household cleaning of a small or moderate accessible area:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>ventilate appropriately</li>
    <li>keep other people away from the work area</li>
    <li>use protective equipment suited to the guidance and person</li>
    <li>follow the cleaning-product label</li>
    <li>protect eyes and skin</li>
    <li>avoid spreading spores</li>
    <li>dry the surface afterwards</li>
    <li>dispose of or wash contaminated cloths as directed</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>dry-brush mould</li>
    <li>sand mouldy material</li>
    <li>use an ordinary vacuum cleaner</li>
    <li>mix cleaning chemicals</li>
    <li>mix bleach with ammonia, acids or other cleaners</li>
    <li>saturate plasterboard, timber or other porous materials</li>
    <li>paint over active mould</li>
    <li>return furnishings before the area is dry</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Where vacuuming is appropriate under official guidance, use equipment fitted with a genuine HEPA filter.</p>
  <p>Respirators need to fit properly and can make breathing harder. People with pre-existing heart or lung conditions should obtain appropriate medical advice before using a P2 respirator.</p>
  <h2>Cleaning products</h2>
  <p>Do not assume bleach is required or suitable for every surface.</p>
  <p>The cleaning method should suit:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>the material</li>
    <li>the extent of growth</li>
    <li>product instructions</li>
    <li>health guidance</li>
    <li>ventilation</li>
    <li>the person completing the work</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Commercial mould products, detergents and other household cleaning methods have different:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>surface restrictions</li>
    <li>ventilation requirements</li>
    <li>protective-equipment instructions</li>
    <li>contact times</li>
    <li>rinsing requirements</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Follow one method at a time.</p>
  <p>Using several products together can create hazardous fumes or damage the surface.</p>
  <p>The moisture source still needs to be corrected regardless of which cleaning product is used.</p>
  <h2>Porous materials</h2>
  <p>Mould can penetrate porous materials such as:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>plasterboard</li>
    <li>carpet</li>
    <li>underlay</li>
    <li>mattresses</li>
    <li>cardboard</li>
    <li>some ceiling tiles</li>
    <li>insulation</li>
    <li>upholstered furniture</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Surface wiping may not remove contamination within the material.</p>
  <p>Items that cannot be cleaned and dried may need professional assessment or disposal.</p>
  <p>Do not discard building materials from a rental or strata property without permission.</p>
  <p>Do not cut out plasterboard or insulation where electrical wiring, asbestos or hidden water damage may be present.</p>
  <h2>Do not paint over it</h2>
  <p>Paint does not remove mould or dry the building.</p>
  <p>Painting over a damp surface can lead to:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>staining</li>
    <li>blistering</li>
    <li>peeling</li>
    <li>trapped moisture</li>
    <li>recurring mould</li>
    <li>concealed damage</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Before repainting:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>stop the moisture source</li>
    <li>clean or remove affected material appropriately</li>
    <li>allow the area to dry</li>
    <li>assess whether the surface remains sound</li>
    <li>use a coating system suitable for the material and room</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A mould-resistant paint may reduce surface growth under some conditions. It does not repair leaks, inadequate ventilation or cold thermal bridges.</p>
  <h2>When professional help is appropriate</h2>
  <p>Professional assessment may be appropriate where:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>growth covers a large area</li>
    <li>mould repeatedly returns</li>
    <li>several rooms are affected</li>
    <li>water damage is extensive</li>
    <li>a leak is concealed</li>
    <li>wall or ceiling materials are damaged</li>
    <li>heating or ventilation systems are contaminated</li>
    <li>sewage or floodwater is involved</li>
    <li>asbestos may be present</li>
    <li>occupants have significant health concerns</li>
    <li>the source cannot be identified</li>
    <li>the building has persistent condensation</li>
    <li>the work requires opening cavities</li>
    <li>the home is undergoing renovation</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Possible professionals may include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>building inspectors</li>
    <li>plumbers</li>
    <li>roof plumbers</li>
    <li>waterproofing specialists</li>
    <li>licensed builders</li>
    <li>occupational hygienists</li>
    <li>ventilation specialists</li>
    <li>mould-remediation professionals</li>
    <li>asbestos professionals</li>
    <li>medical practitioners</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Ask what the person is qualified to assess.</p>
  <p>“Mould specialist” is not a substitute for a clear scope, suitable qualifications and identification of the water source.</p>
  <h2>Mould testing</h2>
  <p>Visible mould and recurring dampness already show that the moisture conditions need attention.</p>
  <p>Testing may be considered in complex situations, but a sample does not repair the problem.</p>
  <p>Before paying for testing, ask:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>What decision will the result change?</li>
    <li>Who collects the sample?</li>
    <li>Is the method appropriate?</li>
    <li>Who interprets the result?</li>
    <li>Will moisture and building conditions also be assessed?</li>
    <li>Does the report identify the source?</li>
    <li>Is remediation independent from testing?</li>
    <li>Are limitations explained?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A report listing mould types without assessing water entry, humidity, ventilation and damaged materials may provide limited practical value.</p>
  <h2>Renters</h2>
  <p>Renters should document:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>where mould appears</li>
    <li>the approximate area</li>
    <li>dates</li>
    <li>photographs</li>
    <li>condensation patterns</li>
    <li>leaks</li>
    <li>failed exhaust fans</li>
    <li>damaged windows</li>
    <li>previous cleaning</li>
    <li>when the problem was reported</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Report promptly:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>roof or plumbing leaks</li>
    <li>failed ventilation</li>
    <li>recurring dampness</li>
    <li>water-damaged materials</li>
    <li>mould that returns after cleaning</li>
    <li>supplied appliances that leak or do not ventilate properly</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not make unauthorised changes to:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>walls</li>
    <li>windows</li>
    <li>exhaust systems</li>
    <li>insulation</li>
    <li>roof spaces</li>
    <li>external seals</li>
    <li>electrical systems</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Follow the tenancy process and requirements in the relevant state or territory.</p>
  <p>Responsibility can depend on the cause, property condition and household use. A generic guide cannot determine a tenancy dispute.</p>
  <h2>Apartments and strata properties</h2>
  <p>Moisture may involve:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>common roofs</li>
    <li>shared walls</li>
    <li>balcony drainage</li>
    <li>common plumbing</li>
    <li>exterior windows</li>
    <li>facade defects</li>
    <li>central ventilation</li>
    <li>neighbouring lots</li>
    <li>fire-rated construction</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Record and report the pattern before arranging invasive work.</p>
  <p>Do not open common walls, modify shared ventilation or alter the facade without approval.</p>
  <p>Water may travel from another lot or common property before appearing inside the apartment.</p>
  <h2>Regional considerations</h2>
  <h3>Cool-temperate and alpine regions</h3>
  <p>Winter priorities may include:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>window condensation</li>
    <li>cold external walls</li>
    <li>insulation</li>
    <li>thermal bridges</li>
    <li>controlled ventilation</li>
    <li>bedroom moisture</li>
    <li>even heating</li>
    <li>drying clothes without loading moisture into the home</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Temperate coastal regions</h3>
  <p>Wind, moderate winter cold and humidity may combine.</p>
  <p>Check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>window seals</li>
    <li>coastal corrosion</li>
    <li>rain penetration</li>
    <li>bathroom exhaust</li>
    <li>furniture spacing</li>
    <li>bedrooms kept closed overnight</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Hot-arid regions</h3>
  <p>Cold winter nights can create condensation at cold surfaces even when daytime conditions are dry.</p>
  <p>Use:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>controlled ventilation</li>
    <li>useful daytime sun</li>
    <li>curtains after sunset</li>
    <li>appropriate room heating</li>
    <li>leak investigation</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Tropical and subtropical regions</h3>
  <p>Winter condensation may be limited, but mould can remain a wet-season or air-conditioning issue.</p>
  <p>Opening the home during very humid outdoor conditions can introduce additional moisture.</p>
  <p>Use ventilation and air conditioning according to actual outdoor conditions rather than assuming open windows always dry the home.</p>
  <h2>A practical investigation sequence</h2>
  <h3>First: record the pattern</h3>
  <p>Note:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>room</li>
    <li>surface</li>
    <li>time of day</li>
    <li>weather</li>
    <li>rainfall</li>
    <li>occupancy</li>
    <li>showering or cooking</li>
    <li>indoor drying</li>
    <li>heating use</li>
    <li>window position</li>
    <li>exhaust-fan use</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Second: control obvious moisture sources</h3>
  <p>Check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>bathroom exhaust</li>
    <li>kitchen exhaust</li>
    <li>dryer type</li>
    <li>indoor drying</li>
    <li>leaks</li>
    <li>damp clothing</li>
    <li>furniture spacing</li>
    <li>morning window water</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Third: improve drying and surface conditions</h3>
  <p>Consider:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>purposeful ventilation</li>
    <li>appropriate heating</li>
    <li>curtains and pelmets</li>
    <li>insulation</li>
    <li>window improvement</li>
    <li>dehumidification</li>
    <li>airflow behind furniture</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Fourth: escalate unresolved problems</h3>
  <p>Arrange suitable assessment where:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list">
    <li>water entry is suspected</li>
    <li>mould is extensive</li>
    <li>material is damaged</li>
    <li>the problem remains unexplained</li>
    <li>household health is affected</li>
    <li>cavities or services are involved</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Before You Buy Moisture-Control Products Checklist</h2>
  <p>Before buying a dehumidifier, mould remover, humidity meter, window product or ventilation device, check:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list" aria-label="Before you buy moisture-control products checklist">
    <li>Is the moisture condensation, a leak or both?</li>
    <li>Does it occur after rain?</li>
    <li>Does it occur after showers, cooking or indoor drying?</li>
    <li>Is the affected surface cold?</li>
    <li>Is the area hidden behind furniture?</li>
    <li>Is the material still sound?</li>
    <li>Is mould extensive or recurring?</li>
    <li>Is concealed growth suspected?</li>
    <li>Is asbestos possible?</li>
    <li>Has the moisture source been identified?</li>
    <li>Does the bathroom fan exhaust effectively?</li>
    <li>Does the rangehood exhaust outside?</li>
    <li>What type of clothes dryer is installed?</li>
    <li>Is dryer moisture reaching the room?</li>
    <li>Can clothes be dried elsewhere?</li>
    <li>Are windows and doors being ventilated purposefully?</li>
    <li>Are required vents still open?</li>
    <li>Are draughts being confused with ventilation?</li>
    <li>Is insulation missing or damaged?</li>
    <li>Are thermal bridges likely?</li>
    <li>Are curtains trapping moisture against glass?</li>
    <li>Can furniture be moved away from external walls?</li>
    <li>Would a dehumidifier operate effectively at the room temperature?</li>
    <li>Can collected water be emptied or drained safely?</li>
    <li>Is the appliance noise acceptable?</li>
    <li>Is a wall power point available without an extension lead?</li>
    <li>Will a humidity meter change a practical decision?</li>
    <li>Is the proposed cleaning product suitable for the surface?</li>
    <li>Are its ventilation and protective-equipment instructions clear?</li>
    <li>Can it be used without mixing chemicals?</li>
    <li>Is professional remediation more appropriate?</li>
    <li>Does a renter or strata occupant need approval?</li>
    <li>Is medical advice appropriate for an exposed household member?</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Winter Condensation and Mould Checklist</h2>
  <p>Check the home systematically:</p>
  <ul class="feature-list" aria-label="Winter condensation and mould checklist">
    <li>Window condensation is wiped and the surface dried.</li>
    <li>Wet cloths are removed from the room to dry.</li>
    <li>Curtains are opened during the day.</li>
    <li>Curtains do not rest against wet glass.</li>
    <li>Window drainage openings remain clear.</li>
    <li>Water entry after rain has been recorded.</li>
    <li>Bathroom exhaust fans operate effectively.</li>
    <li>Bathroom doors do not release moisture through the home unnecessarily.</li>
    <li>Kitchen exhaust is used for moisture-producing cooking.</li>
    <li>Rangehood filters and ducts are maintained.</li>
    <li>Vented-dryer moisture is exhausted appropriately.</li>
    <li>Indoor clothes drying is ventilated.</li>
    <li>No laundry is dried on heaters.</li>
    <li>Plumbing and roof leaks are reported or repaired.</li>
    <li>Gutters and downpipes are checked from ground level.</li>
    <li>Bedrooms are aired appropriately after overnight occupancy.</li>
    <li>Wardrobes contain only dry clothing and shoes.</li>
    <li>Furniture has airflow space from cold external walls.</li>
    <li>Cardboard and soft goods are not stored against damp walls.</li>
    <li>Heating is used appropriately without relying on portable heaters overnight.</li>
    <li>Unflued or outdoor gas appliances are not used as indoor heating.</li>
    <li>Insulation and cold-surface problems are considered.</li>
    <li>Draught proofing has not blocked intentional ventilation.</li>
    <li>Condensation is not being mistaken automatically for a leak.</li>
    <li>A leak is not being dismissed as condensation.</li>
    <li>Small household cleaning follows current health guidance.</li>
    <li>Mould is not dry-brushed.</li>
    <li>An ordinary vacuum is not used on mould.</li>
    <li>Cleaning chemicals are not mixed.</li>
    <li>Affected surfaces are dried after cleaning.</li>
    <li>Paint is not applied over active dampness or mould.</li>
    <li>Damaged porous materials are assessed.</li>
    <li>Extensive or recurring mould is escalated.</li>
    <li>Health concerns are referred for medical advice.</li>
    <li>Rental or strata reports are documented.</li>
    <li>The source is reassessed if mould returns.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Control the moisture, warm the surface and let the room dry</h2>
  <p>Winter mould is not simply a cleaning failure.</p>
  <p>Condensation forms when moisture-laden air reaches a cold surface. Mould can then grow where that moisture remains and materials do not dry.</p>
  <p>Reduce moisture at its source. Use bathroom, kitchen and laundry ventilation effectively. Wipe recurring window water, allow air to circulate behind furniture and address cold surfaces through appropriate heating, insulation and window improvements.</p>
  <p>At the same time, remain alert for leaks and concealed building damage.</p>
  <p>Clean only where the area, surface and household circumstances make that appropriate under current health guidance. Extensive, recurring or concealed mould requires more than a stronger spray.</p>
  <p>The useful result is not a temporarily white wall. It is a home in which the moisture source has been controlled and the affected area can remain dry.</p>

